====Verb====
{{en-verb|fights|fighting|fought|past_ptc2=foughten|past_ptc2_qual=archaic}}

# {{n-g|Senses relating to physical conflict:}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To engage in combat with; to oppose physically, to contest with.
##: {{ux|en|My grandfather '''fought''' the Nazis in World War II.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To conduct or engage in (battle, warfare etc.).
##: {{ux|en|Our soldiers '''fought''' the battle just over that hill.}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay Johnson|passage=was left to '''fight''' his way through the world.}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|2 Timothy|4|7|passage=I have '''fought''' a good fight.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[contend]] in physical conflict, either singly or in war, battle etc.
##: {{ux|en|A wounded animal will '''fight''' tooth and nail; relentless, savage and murderous.}}
## {{lb|en|reciprocal}} To [[contend]] in physical conflict with each other, either singly or in war, battle etc.
##: {{ux|en|The two boxers have been '''fighting''' for more than half an hour.}}
## {{lb|en|causative|archaic}} To cause to fight; to manage or manoeuvre in a fight.
##: {{ux|en|to '''fight''' cocks; to '''fight''' one's ship}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[strive]] for something; to campaign or [[contend]] for success.
#: {{ux|en|He '''fought''' for the Democrats in the last election.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|book=1 Timothy|chapter=6|verse=12|passage='''Fight''' the good fight of faith, lay hold on eternal life, whereunto thou art also called, and hast professed a good profession before many witnesses.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=7|passage=Old Applegate, in the stern, just set and looked at me, and Lord James, amidship, waved both arms and kept hollering for help. I took a couple of everlasting big strokes and managed to grab hold of the skiff's rail, close to the stern. Then, for a jiffy, I hung on and '''fought''' for breath.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2014-07-05|volume=412|issue=8894|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21606252-ideal-political-role-model-freedom-fighter Freedom fighter]
|passage=[Edmund] Burke continued to '''fight''' for liberty later on in life. He backed Americans in their campaign for freedom from British taxation. He supported Catholic freedoms and freer trade with Ireland, in spite of his constituents’ ire. He wanted more liberal laws on the punishment of debtors.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[try]] to [[overpower]]; to [[fiercely]] [[counteract]].
#: {{ux|en|The government pledged to '''fight''' corruption.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=Ann Aguirre|title=The Shape of My Heart|page=42|passage=I '''fought''' a sneeze as Max took my hand and led me into the chapel.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of colours or other design elements: to [[clash]]; to fail to [[harmonize]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Ian G. Clifton|title=Android User Interface Design|passage=The higher the saturation, the more the colors '''fight''', and the more users will be looking at your design instead of your content.}}

=====Usage notes=====
*The reciprocal sense of "fight" is a common ellision of the phrase "fight each other", since context provides the meaning. "Each other" is commonly used with other verbs for an explicit reciprocal sense.
*The reciprocal sense of "fight", which refers to two entities fighting each other, contrasts with the reflexive sense of the word "infight", which refers to entities of a group fighting each other.

